{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"BLOCKED, and it needs an operator decision rather than another retry.\n\n**Where it stands.** ADR 0117 and S10 are finished: plan reviews PASS and are certified on both children, work complete, `make lint` and `make typecheck` exit 0, ratchets `new=0 increased=0`, 1498 tests pass, and both changes are committed locally with `rebar-ticket:` trailers and DCO sign-off. Nothing has been pushed to Gerrit, because neither ticket is claimed.\n\n**Why they cannot be claimed.** `claim` cascades parent-first, so both children need this epic claimable, and this epic needs its own certified plan-review attestation. That attestation cannot be earned right now:\n\n```\nrebar sign-review 55da-cee1-e556-4735\n  verdict: stale-pin-drift\n  reason: a dependency's plan material changed since the review (4cfc-2de2-747d-4388),\n          so the recorded PASS no longer reflects it — run `rebar review-plan` to\n          re-review and sign\n```\n\nAn epic review pins every child's material. **S1 (`friendless-alabaster-cub`, `4cfc-2de2-747d-4388`) is being actively edited by another session**, so its fingerprint moves faster than a ~10-minute epic review completes. Observed across three checks: pinned `9277ed2b1dc4dd41`, then current `b4ed31dc1052dcaf`, then `bd18d3bff25b5706` a few minutes later. The other fourteen children are all `current`; S1 alone is the drift.\n\nThe epic's own review does PASS on its merits (`verdict: PASS`, blocking 0) after the readiness-floor remediation — the signature simply cannot be minted while a pinned dependency keeps moving. This is the documented \"review dependencies FIRST, never review a ticket and its dependencies in parallel\" hazard, and it is not avoidable from this side: I do not control when S1's author stops editing.\n\n**Not doing:** `claim --force`. It would bypass the start-work gate on this epic and both children, and the whole audit value of the gate is that a forced op records no certification. An agent must not make that call.\n\n**Options for the operator, cheapest first:**\n\n1. **Wait for a quiet window on S1**, then run `review-plan` on this epic once and claim immediately. The window has to be shorter than one epic review (~10 min here). This costs one review and no judgment.\n2. **Claim this epic while S1 is settled**, and leave it `in_progress` — once claimed, later S1 edits no longer block the children, because the cascade only needs the parent already in progress.\n3. **Force the epic claim** with a reason, if the drift window never closes. Human-only.\n\nOption 2 is probably the real fix: this epic is going to be worked for weeks by several sessions, and leaving it `open` means every child claim re-races every sibling's in-flight plan edits.\n\n**Two unrelated infrastructure faults seen while doing this,** worth knowing about because both cost real time and both produce misleading signals:\n\n- **Gate daemons keep dying.** Four `review_plan_start` runs returned `stale-running` — the daemon died before recording a terminal status — which reads identically to \"still running\" until you poll `gate_status`. It lines up with this host's hourly LaunchAgent that pipx-reinstalls `rebar`/`rebar-mcp` from `main`; the MCP server does not hot-reload, so a restart mid-review orphans the run. One MCP call also died with `transport dropped mid-call`.\n- **The tracker store reported itself DIVERGED for several minutes,** then recovered on its own. `rebar fsck` reports it as a standing condition, not a blip:\n\n```\n{\"kind\":\"diverged\",\"detail\":\"local 'tickets' branch has diverged from origin/tickets — no shared\n history / cannot fast-forward ... its writes will never push. Recover: run `rebar fsck-recover`,\n or re-clone and re-init\"}\n{\"kind\":\"env_id_mismatch\",\"detail\":\"this environment has also written events under 2 other\n environment id(s) ... Op-cert attestations signed by those identities cannot be verified here\n (`foreign_key` at the claim/close gates) and must be re-earned. This is the signature of a\n tracker re-clone that did not carry over its git-ignored local state.\"}\n```\n\n31 issues total, including 20 `orphan_event` entries. I have not run `fsck-recover` or `tracker-maintenance`: it rewrites a shared store that another session is actively writing to, and that is an operator call, not an agent's. Flagging it because \"writes will never push\" would mean ticket events silently not reaching `origin/tickets` — worth confirming before trusting today's tracker history."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788580637321180701,"uuid":"a2b90c32-7e1a-4d0c-a245-b7708626bbe0"}